WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Least privilege is not a one-time setting.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:10.120
Scripts change, systems change, operators change, and access accumulates.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.940
A permission that was narrow last year may be too broad after the script grows.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.640
A service account that once supported one workflow may now be reused by several unrelated jobs.

5
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.660
Without logging, monitoring, and review, least privilege slowly turns into inherited privilege.

6
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:38.140
Useful logs should show enough operational context to support review: which identity ran, what high-level action occurred, what target was affected, when it happened, and whether it succeeded.

7
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:46.320
Logs should avoid secrets, tokens, private keys, sensitive file contents, and excessive sensitive output.

8
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:51.940
The goal is accountability and troubleshooting without turning logs into a second data exposure problem.

9
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:57.260
Monitoring should look for signals that privilege boundaries may be wrong or changing.

10
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.560
Permission failures can indicate a script is trying to do more than its design allows.

11
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:15.060
Unexpected execution locations, unusual target systems, sudden requests for broader access, repeated manual overrides, or scripts running under surprising identities are all worth attention.

12
00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.040
These signals are not automatically incidents, but they are useful prompts for review.

13
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:35.280
Periodic access review should examine service accounts, local users, groups, file permissions, sudo rules, tokens, SSH keys, CI/CD variables, scheduled jobs, service definitions, and remote access paths.

14
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:45.300
ShellCheck and peer review help before access is granted by catching risky script patterns, unclear quoting, fragile assumptions, and confusing control flow.

15
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:53.160
The long-term habit is continuous improvement: remove stale access, narrow broad permissions, and make privileged automation easier to explain.

16
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:59.420
Permission failures and unusual access patterns provide concrete prompts for access review.
